## Pattern 3: Reversal and rotation {#sec-arr-p3}

**From one direction to two.** In Pattern 2 the two indices moved in the *same* direction, a writer trailing a reader. Pattern 3 keeps the goal (rewrite in place, $O(1)$ extra space) but sends the indices in *opposite* directions: one starts at each end, and every step swaps the two cells they point at. That reverses a range (@sec-arr-inplace), and three reversals move whole blocks: a rotation.

**Recognise it.** The statement says **reverse**, **rotate**, **cyclic shift**, "move the last $k$ elements to the front", "shift every element $k$ places to the right, wrapping around". Two blocks must **swap places** while each keeps its inner order ($AB \to BA$): that is a rotation, whatever the story says. You must decide whether an array is a rotation of another one, or of a sorted array. Many rotations are applied but only a few cells are read afterwards: keep a start offset instead of moving data. The follow-up asks for $O(1)$ extra memory.

**When not.** If elements must be reordered *by value*, that is sorting (@sec-srt). A *search* in a rotated sorted array is binary search (@sec-bst). Rotations of a changing subarray, applied many times, need a balanced tree.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
**Rotating right by $k$** (the element at index $i$ moves to $(i + k) \bmod n$; the last $k$ elements come to the front) is three reversals. First reduce `k %= n`, then:

```text
reverse(a, 0, n)        # whole array:      AB   -> B^R A^R
reverse(a, 0, k)        # the front block:  B^R  -> B
reverse(a, k, n)        # the back block:   A^R  -> A
```

Each `reverse(l, r)` is the two-pointer swap loop of @sec-arr-inplace on the half-open range $[l, r)$.
:::

**Why it is correct.** Write the array as two blocks, $A$ = the first $n - k$ elements and $B$ = the last $k$; we want $AB \to BA$. Let $X^R$ denote $X$ reversed. Reversing a concatenation turns the order of the blocks around **and** reverses each block, and reversing twice is the identity:

$$(AB)^R = B^R A^R, \qquad (X^R)^R = X.$$ {#eq-arr-rev}

So reversing the whole array gives $B^R A^R$: the blocks are now in the right places, each backwards. Reversing the first $k$ cells turns $B^R$ into $B$, and reversing the last $n - k$ turns $A^R$ into $A$: the result is $BA$. $\blacksquare$

**Rotating left by $k$** is rotating right by $n - k$. With reversals it is the same three calls in the other order: reverse $[0, k)$, reverse $[k, n)$, then reverse all ($A^R B^R$ with $|A| = k$, then $(A^R B^R)^R = BA$). Always reduce $k \bmod n$ first: rotating by $n$ is the identity, and $k$ may be much larger than $n$.

**Complexity.** The three reversals do $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor + \lfloor k/2 \rfloor + \lfloor (n-k)/2 \rfloor \le n$ swaps: $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ extra space. The C++ library has `std::reverse(first, last)` and `std::rotate(first, middle, last)`; the latter makes `middle` the new first element, a **left** rotation by `middle - first`.

