**Looking back.** Every pattern was the same walk over contiguous memory, carrying a little more state each time: a few variables (aggregates), a write pointer that turns the prefix into the output (compaction), a second pointer coming from the other end (reversal and rotation), an index computed by a rule (simulation), and a second array indexed by value (frequency arrays). Each was proved the same way, with an invariant about the processed prefix $[0, i)$. The next chapters keep that walk and grow the state once more: a running total that answers range questions (@sec-pref), a hash map for values too large to index (@sec-hash), and two pointers that move under a rule (@sec-tp, @sec-sw).

## Pattern map

| Signal in the statement | Pattern | Section |
|---|---|---|
| Answer is one summary of all cells (max, count, top-$k$, longest streak) | Single-pass aggregates | @sec-arr-p1 |
| A per-position answer needs one global fact first ("max of the others") | Two passes over running state | @sec-arr-scan, @sec-arr-p1-b |
| Best pair $i < j$ with an extreme partner ("largest $a_j - a_i$") | Prefix extreme as a running variable | @sec-arr-p1 |
| "Modify in place", keep order, remove or compact, return the new length | Read/write pointers | @sec-arr-p2 |
| Sorted input, "at most $k$ copies" | Read/write, compare with `a[w - k]` | @sec-arr-p2-b |
| Output longer than the input (duplicate, insert) in place | Read/write from the back | @sec-arr-inplace, @sec-arr-p2 |
| Rotate by $k$, cyclic shift, "two blocks swap places" | Three reversals ($k \bmod n$ first) | @sec-arr-p3 |
| Many rotations, few reads | Start offset, $(start + i) \bmod n$ | @sec-arr-p3 |
| "Is it a rotation of …", "sorted and rotated" | Windows of $aa$; count circular drops | @sec-arr-p3 |
| Number as a digit array; "add one" | Carry from the least significant end | @sec-arr-p4 |
| Index rule: $a[a[i]]$, $2i$, $i \bmod n$, $rC + c$ | Simulation and index arithmetic | @sec-arr-p4 |
| A new value per cell from old values, $O(1)$ space | Encode two values in one cell | @sec-arr-inplace, @sec-arr-p4-b |
| Values in a small range; "how many smaller"; counting sort | Frequency array + running sum | @sec-arr-p5 |
| "Are the counts distinct?", "how many values occur $k$ times?" | Frequency of frequencies | @sec-arr-p5-b |
| Values up to $10^9$ | Not a counting array: hash map or sort | @sec-hash, @sec-srt |

## Pitfalls

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **A maximum started at `0`** fails on all-negative input; start from the first element, or from a sentinel outside the value range (@sec-arr-p1).
- **A sentinel that can occur in the data**: `INT_MIN` as "unset" cannot tell "unset" from a real $-2^{31}$; use a wider type, `None`, or a flag (@sec-arr-p1-a).
- **Letting a duplicate take a rank** in a top-$k$ of *distinct* values, or skipping duplicates when the statement counts elements (@sec-arr-p1-a, @sec-arr-p1-b).
- **Updating the prefix state before using it** in a pair question pairs $a_j$ with itself (@sec-arr-p1).
- **Off-by-one on ranges**: `<=` where you meant `<`, `range(n + 1)` reading `a[n]`, `v.size() - 1` on an empty vector (@sec-arr-ranges).
- **`int` overflow** on a running sum, a pair count, or `int * int`: widen to `long long` *before* the operation (@sec-arr-cost).
- **Clearing a big array in every test case** instead of only the cells you touched: $t \cdot V$ work (@sec-arr-multitest).
- **Destroying data you still need** while writing in place: keep `w <= r`, choose the loop direction, or encode (@sec-arr-inplace).
- **Comparing with the read side** (`a[r - 2]`) in Remove Duplicates II: that cell may already be overwritten; compare with `a[w - 2]` (@sec-arr-p2).
- **`erase` inside a loop**: $O(n^2)$, and the element after each erase is skipped (@sec-arr-inplace).
- **`k` without `% n`** in rotation, or the three reversals in the wrong order, which rotates left instead of right (@sec-arr-p3-a).
- **Linear instead of circular drops** in "sorted and rotated": $[2,1,3,4]$ has one linear drop but is not a rotated sorted array (@sec-arr-p3-b).
- **Negative `%` in C++**: `-7 % 5 == -2`; use `((i % n) + n) % n` (@sec-arr-p4).
- **Reading an encoded cell without `% n`**, or an encoding whose $n^2$ overflows `int` (@sec-arr-p4-b).
- **Rows and columns mixed up** in $r\cdot C + c$: square test grids hide the bug (@sec-arr-2d).
- **A counting array one cell too short** ($\text{MAX}$ instead of $\text{MAX} + 1$), or **indexed by a negative value** without an offset (@sec-arr-p5).
- **Counting the zero counters** when checking "are all counts distinct" (@sec-arr-p5-b).
- **Assuming a contiguous array is always fast**: only in-order access is cache-friendly; scattered jumps lose most of the advantage (@sec-arr-cache).
:::


## Exercises

The exercises follow the chapter: the lessons first (1–6), then one block per pattern (7–13 aggregates, 14–19 read/write pointers, 20–26 reversal and rotation, 27–32 simulation, 33–37 frequency arrays), then three harder in-place problems (38–40). Every solution that has code is checked here against a brute force or the official samples.

**1. [E] Why is `a[i]` $O(1)$ in an array but reaching the $i$-th node of a linked list $O(i)$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The array stores cell $i$ at the address `base + i·s` (@eq-arr-address), computable with one multiplication and one addition. A linked list only stores, in each node, where the *next* node is; the address of node $i$ cannot be derived without visiting nodes $0, \dots, i-1$ and following $i$ pointers. Each of those hops also goes to an address the processor cannot predict, so it often misses the cache (@sec-arr-cache).
:::

**2. [E] How many elements are in the half-open range $[3, 9)$? In $[k, k)$? Write the closed range $[2, 7]$ as a half-open one and give its length.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$9 - 3 = 6$ elements (indices $3, \dots, 8$). $[k, k)$ is empty, $k - k = 0$, which is why `while lo < hi` stops exactly on the empty range. The closed $[2, 7]$ is the half-open $[2, 8)$, length $8 - 2 = 6 = 7 - 2 + 1$ (@sec-arr-ranges).
:::

**3. [E] How many elements are copied in total by $9$ `push_back` calls into an empty doubling vector? By $1000$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Copies happen at the pushes that find the buffer full: the push after sizes $1, 2, 4, 8, \dots$. For $9$ pushes that is $1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 < 18$ (@fig-arr-anim-grow). For $1000$ pushes, $1 + 2 + \dots + 512 = 1023 < 2000$, as `copies_for` checks in @sec-arr-vec. In general the total is below $2n$.
:::

**4. [M] Show that growing a full buffer by a constant $c$ cells makes $n$ appends cost $\Theta(n^2/c)$, while growing it by a factor $g > 1$ keeps them $O(n)$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
*Additive.* The buffer is full at sizes $c, 2c, 3c, \dots$, and each time all elements are copied, so $n$ appends copy $c + 2c + \dots + \lfloor (n-1)/c \rfloor c \approx \frac{n^2}{2c}$ elements: quadratic ($49\,500$ copies for $n = 1000$, $c = 10$, as checked in @sec-arr-vec). *Multiplicative.* The capacities at the copies grow like $1, g, g^2, \dots$ up to the last one below $n$, so their sum is at most $n(1 + 1/g + 1/g^2 + \dots) = \frac{g}{g-1}n$: $2n$ for $g = 2$, $3n$ for $g = 1.5$. That is $O(n)$ in total, $O(1)$ amortised per append.
:::

**5. [E] A grid has $R = 3$ rows and $C = 5$ columns, stored row-major in one array. Which id does cell $(2, 4)$ get, and which cell has id $7$? What goes wrong if you divide by $R$ instead?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$(2, 4) \to 2\cdot5 + 4 = 14$, the last cell. Id $7 \to (\lfloor 7/5 \rfloor, 7 \bmod 5) = (1, 2)$. Dividing by $R = 3$ gives $(2, 1)$, a different cell; the bug is invisible on square grids, where $R = C$, so always test index code on a non-square example (@sec-arr-2d).

::: {#edae4e09 .cell execution_count=71}
``` {.python .cell-code}
ex5_R, ex5_C = 3, 5
assert 2 * ex5_C + 4 == 14 and (7 // ex5_C, 7 % ex5_C) == (1, 2) and (7 // ex5_R, 7 % ex5_R) == (2, 1)
```
:::


:::

**6. [M] State and prove the invariant of `count_max_one_pass` from @sec-arr-scan.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
*Invariant:* before processing $a_i$, `best` $= \max(a[0, i))$ and `cnt` is the number of occurrences of that maximum in $a[0, i)$, with the convention that for $i = 0$ we start from `best` $= a_0$, `cnt` $= 0$ (and $a_0$ is then counted by the first iteration, since it equals `best`). *Maintenance:* if $a_i >$ `best`, the new maximum is $a_i$ and it occurs once in $a[0, i]$: `(best, cnt) = (a_i, 1)`. If $a_i =$ `best`, the maximum is unchanged and one more copy appears: `cnt + 1`. If $a_i <$ `best`, nothing changes. *Termination:* at $i = n$ the pair is the maximum of the whole array and its count. The stress test in @sec-arr-scan checks the code against the two-pass version.
:::

**7. [E] [LC 485 · Max Consecutive Ones](https://leetcode.com/problems/max-consecutive-ones/){difficulty="Easy"}. Return the length of the longest block of consecutive 1s in a binary array ($n \le 10^5$).**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
State: `run` = the length of the block of 1s ending at the current position, `best` = the longest block so far. A 1 extends the block, a 0 resets it. The answer must be updated *inside* the loop: reading `run` only at the end returns the length of the *last* block.

::: {#4aa79cf3 .cell execution_count=72}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex7_max_ones(a):
    run = best = 0
    for x in a:
        run = run + 1 if x == 1 else 0
        best = max(best, run)
    return best

assert ex7_max_ones([1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1]) == 3 and ex7_max_ones([1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1]) == 2
for _ in range(500):
    e7 = [random.randint(0, 1) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 12))]
    assert ex7_max_ones(e7) == max((r - l for l in range(len(e7)) for r in range(l, len(e7) + 1)
                                    if all(e7[l:r])), default=0)
```
:::


:::

**8. [E] [CF 1676B · Equal Candies](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1676/B){rating="800"}. $n \le 50$ boxes hold $a_i \le 10^7$ candies. You may only *eat* candies. Find the minimum number eaten so that all boxes end equal.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The final level $L$ cannot exceed any box, so $L \le \min a_i$, and eating more than necessary only costs, so $L = \min a_i$. The answer is $\sum_i (a_i - L) = S - n\cdot\min a_i$: a sum and a minimum, one pass. Here $50 \cdot 10^7$ still fits in an `int`, but the `long long` habit costs nothing.

::: {#1f5925ec .cell execution_count=73}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex8_candies(a):
    s, mn = 0, a[0]
    for x in a:
        s, mn = s + x, min(mn, x)
    return s - len(a) * mn

assert ex8_candies([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 10 and ex8_candies([1, 1, 1]) == 0
for _ in range(300):
    e8 = [random.randint(1, 20) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 8))]
    assert ex8_candies(e8) == min(sum(x - L for x in e8) for L in range(0, min(e8) + 1))
```
:::


:::

**9. [E] [LC 2016 · Maximum Difference Between Increasing Elements](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-difference-between-increasing-elements/){difficulty="Easy"}. Return the maximum of $a_j - a_i$ over $i < j$ with $a_i < a_j$, or $-1$ if there is none.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
For each $j$ the best $i < j$ is the one with the smallest $a_i$: keep `lo` $= \min(a_0..a_{j-1})$, use it *before* adding $a_j$, and only when $a_j >$ `lo` (strictly), otherwise the pair is not increasing. On $[5, 5]$ an unconditional `best = max(best, a[j] - lo)` would return $0$ instead of $-1$.

::: {#ea6ffcb7 .cell execution_count=74}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex9_max_diff(a):
    lo, best = a[0], -1
    for x in a[1:]:
        if x > lo:
            best = max(best, x - lo)          # pair (argmin of the prefix, j)
        lo = min(lo, x)                       # then include a[j]
    return best

assert [ex9_max_diff(t) for t in ([7, 1, 5, 4], [9, 4, 3, 2], [1, 5, 2, 10], [5, 5])] == [4, -1, 9, -1]
for _ in range(500):
    e9 = [random.randint(1, 9) for _ in range(random.randint(2, 8))]
    assert ex9_max_diff(e9) == max([e9[j] - e9[i] for i in range(len(e9)) for j in range(i + 1, len(e9))
                                    if e9[i] < e9[j]], default=-1)
```
:::


:::

**10. [E] [CF 1979A · Guess the Maximum](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1979/A){rating="800"}. Alice picks $k$; Bob then picks $i < j$ and takes $M = \max(a_i, \dots, a_j)$; Alice wins if $M > k$. Find the largest $k$ for which Alice always wins.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Bob wants $M$ as small as possible, and extending a segment can only raise its maximum, so his best choices are segments of length two. Alice wins iff $k < \min_i \max(a_i, a_{i+1})$, so the answer is that minimum minus one: an aggregate over adjacent pairs. The traps are thinking about the global maximum, or forgetting that Bob must take at least two elements.

::: {#7f1a5fea .cell execution_count=75}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex10_guess(a):
    return min(max(a[i], a[i + 1]) for i in range(len(a) - 1)) - 1

assert ex10_guess([2, 4, 1, 7]) == 3
for _ in range(500):
    e10 = [random.randint(1, 9) for _ in range(random.randint(2, 7))]
    assert ex10_guess(e10) == min(max(e10[i:j + 1]) for i in range(len(e10))
                                  for j in range(i + 1, len(e10))) - 1
```
:::


