**14. [E] [LC 1089 · Duplicate Zeros](https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-zeros/){difficulty="Easy"}. Duplicate every zero of a fixed-length array in place, shifting the rest right; elements pushed past the end are lost.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The output is longer than the kept input, so a forward write pointer would run ahead of the read pointer: on $[1, 0, 2, 3]$ it produces $[1, 0, 0, 0]$. Count the zeros $z$. Without truncation, element $i$ would land at $i + (\text{zeros before } i)$, so walk $i$ from the right with a *virtual* write position $j$ starting at $n - 1 + z$; write only when $j < n$, and write a zero twice. Since $j \ge i$ always, no unread cell is overwritten: the mirror image of $w \le r$ (@sec-arr-p2).

::: {#74bd6222 .cell execution_count=79}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex14_duplicate_zeros(a):
    n, j = len(a), len(a) - 1 + a.count(0)
    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
        if j < n:
            a[j] = a[i]
        if a[i] == 0:                         # a zero is written twice
            j -= 1
            if j < n:
                a[j] = 0
        j -= 1
    return a

assert ex14_duplicate_zeros([1, 0, 2, 3, 0, 4, 5, 0]) == [1, 0, 0, 2, 3, 0, 0, 4]
for _ in range(1000):
    e14 = [random.choice([0, 0, 1, 2, 3]) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 9))]
    want = [y for x in e14 for y in ([0, 0] if x == 0 else [x])][:len(e14)]
    assert ex14_duplicate_zeros(list(e14)) == want
```
