**15. [E] [CF 1385B · Restore the Permutation by Merger](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1385/B){rating="800"}. A permutation $p$ of $1..n$ was merged with a copy of itself, keeping the order within each copy. Restore $p$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Each value's first occurrence in the merged sequence comes from one of the two copies, and in both copies the values appear in the order of $p$; so the first occurrences appear in the order of $p$. The answer is the subsequence of first occurrences: a read/write filter whose keep rule is a `seen` array over the small values $1..n$ (Pattern 5 used as a filter).

::: {#b7a8909b .cell execution_count=80}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex15_restore(a, n):
    seen, w = [False] * (n + 1), 0
    a = list(a)
    for r in range(len(a)):
        if not seen[a[r]]:
            seen[a[r]] = True
            a[w] = a[r]
            w += 1
    return a[:w]

for _ in range(500):
    e15_n = random.randint(1, 7)
    e15_p = random.sample(range(1, e15_n + 1), e15_n)
    picks = sorted(random.sample(range(2 * e15_n), e15_n))       # positions taken by the first copy
    merged, it1, it2 = [], iter(e15_p), iter(e15_p)
    for pos in range(2 * e15_n):
        merged.append(next(it1) if pos in picks else next(it2))
    assert ex15_restore(merged, e15_n) == e15_p
```
