**16. [E] [LC 3174 · Clear Digits](https://leetcode.com/problems/clear-digits/){difficulty="Easy"}. Repeatedly delete the first digit together with the closest non-digit character to its left. Return the final string.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The closest letter to the left that is still present is exactly the last *kept* letter, so the kept prefix is a stack: a letter is pushed (`s[w++] = c`), a digit pops (`w--`). This is keep rule (c), cancellation, of @sec-arr-p2. On `ab1c2d`: push a, b; the 1 pops b; push c; the 2 pops c; push d: `ad`.

::: {#61c7bcac .cell execution_count=81}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex16_clear(s):
    s, w = list(s), 0
    for c in s:
        if c.isdigit():
            w = max(w - 1, 0)                 # the digit deletes the last kept letter
        else:
            s[w] = c
            w += 1
    return "".join(s[:w])

def ex16_brute(s):
    while any(c.isdigit() for c in s):
        d = next(i for i, c in enumerate(s) if c.isdigit())
        l = max(i for i in range(d) if not s[i].isdigit())
        s = s[:l] + s[l + 1:d] + s[d + 1:]
    return s

assert [ex16_clear(t) for t in ("abc", "cb34", "ab1c2d")] == ["abc", "", "ad"]
for _ in range(500):
    e16, letters = "", 0
    for _ in range(random.randint(1, 10)):
        if letters and random.random() < 0.4:
            e16, letters = e16 + random.choice("0123456789"), letters - 1
        else:
            e16, letters = e16 + random.choice("abc"), letters + 1
    assert ex16_clear(e16) == ex16_brute(e16)
```
