**19. [M] Delete a list of positions.** Given an array of length $n$ and a *sorted* list of $m$ distinct positions, delete those positions in place in $O(n + m)$ time and $O(1)$ extra memory, keeping the order of the rest.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Two read pointers and one write pointer: $r$ scans the array, $j$ scans the positions. If `pos[j] == r`, index $r$ is deleted and $j$ advances; otherwise $a_r$ is copied to $a_w$. Because the positions are sorted, `pos[j]` is always the smallest position not yet passed. Deleting with `erase` one by one would cost $O(nm)$ and would also shift every later position.

::: {#292e7efc .cell execution_count=84}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex19_delete(a, pos):
    w = j = 0
    for r in range(len(a)):
        if j < len(pos) and pos[j] == r:
            j += 1                              # r is deleted
            continue
        a[w], w = a[r], w + 1
    return w

e19 = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]
assert e19[:ex19_delete(e19, [0, 2, 3])] == [20, 50, 60]
for _ in range(500):
    e19 = list(range(random.randint(0, 9)))
    e19_pos = sorted(random.sample(range(len(e19)), random.randint(0, len(e19))))
    want = [x for i, x in enumerate(e19) if i not in e19_pos]
    assert e19[:ex19_delete(e19, e19_pos)] == want
```
