**21. [E] [LC 2946 · Matrix Similarity After Cyclic Shifts](https://leetcode.com/problems/matrix-similarity-after-cyclic-shifts/){difficulty="Easy"}. In each of $k$ steps the even-indexed rows shift one place left and the odd-indexed rows one place right, cyclically. Is the final matrix equal to the original?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
After $k$ steps an even row is rotated left by $k$ and an odd row right by $k$; no need to simulate. A row equals its left rotation by $k$ iff `row[j] == row[(j + k) % n]` for every $j$, and that holds iff it equals its *right* rotation by $k$ (substitute $j \to j - k$). So the direction is a red herring: $O(mn)$ time, $O(1)$ memory.

::: {#31afbc95 .cell execution_count=86}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex21_similar(mat, k):
    return all(row[j] == row[(j + k) % len(row)] for row in mat for j in range(len(row)))

def ex21_brute(mat, k):
    cur = [row[:] for row in mat]
    for _ in range(k):
        cur = [row[1:] + row[:1] if i % 2 == 0 else row[-1:] + row[:-1] for i, row in enumerate(cur)]
    return cur == mat

assert not ex21_similar([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]], 4)
assert ex21_similar([[1, 2, 1, 2], [5, 5, 5, 5], [6, 3, 6, 3]], 2)
for _ in range(500):
    e21_n = random.randint(1, 4)
    e21 = [[random.randint(1, 2) for _ in range(e21_n)] for _ in range(random.randint(1, 3))]
    e21_k = random.randint(1, 6)
    assert ex21_similar(e21, e21_k) == ex21_brute(e21, e21_k)
```
