**22. [M] [CF 1726A · Mainak and Array](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1726/A){rating="900"}. Perform exactly one operation: choose a subarray and rotate it cyclically by any amount. Maximise $a_n - a_1$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
A subarray containing neither end changes nothing. One containing only the last end, but not the first, can bring any $a_i$ with $i \ge 2$ to the end: value $\max_{i \ge 2} a_i - a_1$. One containing only the first end can bring any $a_i$ with $i \le n-1$ to the front: $a_n - \min_{i \le n-1} a_i$. Rotating the whole array makes the new ends two old neighbours $a_{i-1}$ (last) and $a_i$ (first): $\max_i (a_{i-1} - a_i)$, plus the rotation by $0$, $a_n - a_1$. The answer is the best of these four aggregates.

::: {#dcc658a2 .cell execution_count=87}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex22_mainak(a):
    n = len(a)
    best = a[-1] - a[0]
    if n > 1:
        best = max(best, max(a[1:]) - a[0], a[-1] - min(a[:-1]),
                   max(a[i - 1] - a[i] for i in range(1, n)))
    return best

def ex22_brute(a):
    best = -math.inf
    for l in range(len(a)):
        for r in range(l + 1, len(a) + 1):
            for s in range(r - l):
                b = a[:l] + a[l:r][s:] + a[l:r][:s] + a[r:]
                best = max(best, b[-1] - b[0])
    return best

assert [ex22_mainak(t) for t in ([1, 3, 9, 11, 5, 7], [20], [9, 99, 999], [2, 1, 8, 1])] == [10, 0, 990, 7]
for _ in range(500):
    e22 = [random.randint(1, 9) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 6))]
    assert ex22_mainak(e22) == ex22_brute(e22)
```
