**23. [M] [LC 396 · Rotate Function](https://leetcode.com/problems/rotate-function/){difficulty="Medium"}. For the array $B_k$ obtained by rotating $a$ right by $k$, let $F(k) = \sum_i i\cdot B_k[i]$. Return $\max_k F(k)$ in $O(n)$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Let $S = \sum a_i$. From $B_{k-1}$ to $B_k$ every element moves one index right, adding $S$ in total, except the element that was at index $n - 1$, namely $a[n - k]$, which wraps to index $0$: it contributed $(n-1)a[n-k]$, was counted once more in $S$, and now contributes $0$. Hence $F(k) = F(k-1) + S - n\cdot a[n-k]$: a running update instead of $n$ fresh sums. Intermediate values reach about $10^5 \cdot 10^5 \cdot 100 = 10^{12}$, so use `long long` in C++.

::: {#3dc8c277 .cell execution_count=88}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex23_rotate_function(a):
    n, S = len(a), sum(a)
    F = sum(i * x for i, x in enumerate(a))
    best = F
    for k in range(1, n):
        F += S - n * a[n - k]                 # a[n - k] wraps from the end to index 0
        best = max(best, F)
    return best

assert ex23_rotate_function([4, 3, 2, 6]) == 26 and ex23_rotate_function([100]) == 0
for _ in range(500):
    e23 = [random.randint(-5, 5) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 7))]
    rots = [e23[len(e23) - k:] + e23[:len(e23) - k] for k in range(len(e23))]
    assert ex23_rotate_function(e23) == max(sum(i * x for i, x in enumerate(b)) for b in rots)
```
