**24. [E] [LC 1460 · Make Two Arrays Equal by Reversing Subarrays](https://leetcode.com/problems/make-two-arrays-equal-by-reversing-subarrays/){difficulty="Easy"}. In one step you may reverse any subarray of `arr`. Can it be made equal to `target`?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
It looks like a search over sequences of reversals, but reversing a subarray of length 2 swaps two neighbours, and adjacent swaps reach *every* arrangement (that is how bubble sort works). Reversals can do no more than permute. So the answer is "yes" iff both arrays hold the same values with the same multiplicities, which a counting array over the values $\le 1000$ decides in $O(n + 1000)$ (Pattern 5). The cell confirms the claim by searching every sequence of reversals on small arrays.

::: {#9ae300eb .cell execution_count=89}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex24_can_equal(target, arr):
    cnt = [0] * 1001
    for x in target:
        cnt[x] += 1
    for x in arr:
        cnt[x] -= 1
        if cnt[x] < 0:
            return False
    return len(arr) == len(target)

def ex24_reachable(arr):
    seen, todo = {tuple(arr)}, [tuple(arr)]
    while todo:
        cur = todo.pop()
        for l in range(len(cur)):
            for r in range(l + 1, len(cur) + 1):
                nxt = cur[:l] + cur[l:r][::-1] + cur[r:]
                if nxt not in seen:
                    seen.add(nxt)
                    todo.append(nxt)
    return seen

for _ in range(100):
    e24 = [random.randint(1, 3) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 5))]
    e24_t = [random.randint(1, 3) for _ in range(len(e24))]
    assert ex24_can_equal(e24_t, e24) == (tuple(e24_t) in ex24_reachable(e24))
```
