**25. [M] [CF 451B · Sort the Array](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/451/B){rating="1300"}. Given $n$ *distinct* integers, decide whether reversing exactly one segment makes the array increasing, and if so print one.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Let $\ell$ be the first index with $a_\ell > a_{\ell+1}$ (if there is none the array is sorted; reverse $[1, 1]$). Everything before $\ell$ is already in place, so a valid segment must start at $\ell$, and it must contain the whole decreasing run starting there: extend $r$ while $a_r > a_{r+1}$. Reverse $[\ell, r]$ and check *once* whether the array is now sorted. The trap is to skip that final check: $[3, 1, 2, 4]$ reverses its run $3, 1$ into $[1, 3, 2, 4]$, still unsorted, so the answer is "no".

::: {#51042ab4 .cell execution_count=90}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex25_sort_by_reversal(a):
    n, l = len(a), 0
    while l + 1 < n and a[l] < a[l + 1]:
        l += 1
    if l == n - 1:
        return (1, 1)
    r = l
    while r + 1 < n and a[r] > a[r + 1]:
        r += 1
    b = a[:l] + a[l:r + 1][::-1] + a[r + 1:]
    return (l + 1, r + 1) if b == sorted(a) else None     # 1-based, as the judge prints

def ex25_brute_exists(a):
    return any(a[:l] + a[l:r][::-1] + a[r:] == sorted(a) for l in range(len(a)) for r in range(l + 1, len(a) + 1))

assert ex25_sort_by_reversal([3, 2, 1]) == (1, 3) and ex25_sort_by_reversal([3, 1, 2, 4]) is None
for _ in range(1000):
    e25 = random.sample(range(10), random.randint(1, 7))
    got = ex25_sort_by_reversal(e25)
    assert (got is not None) == ex25_brute_exists(e25)
    if got:
        l, r = got[0] - 1, got[1]
        assert e25[:l] + e25[l:r][::-1] + e25[r:] == sorted(e25)
```
