**26. [M] Rotate an array **left** by $k$ with three reversals. Check your order on $n = 3$, $k = 1$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Left by $k$ is right by $n - k$, or the same three reversals in the other order: reverse $[0, k)$, reverse $[k, n)$, reverse all. With $A$ the first $k$ elements: $AB \to A^R B^R \to (A^R B^R)^R = BA$ by @eq-arr-rev. On $[1, 2, 3]$, $k = 1$: $[1, 2, 3] \to [1, 3, 2] \to [2, 3, 1]$, the left rotation. The "right" order (reverse all first) would give $[3, 1, 2]$, the right rotation: test a tiny case before submitting.

