**29. [E] [CF 1873B · Good Kid](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1873/B){rating="800"}. Given at most $9$ digits, add $1$ to exactly one of them so that the product of all digits is as large as possible.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Adding 1 to digit $d$ multiplies the product by $(d+1)/d$, which is largest for the smallest $d$; if some digit is $0$, raising one $0$ to $1$ is the only way to get a non-zero product. Either way: add 1 to the **smallest** digit, an aggregate (the minimum) followed by one product.

::: {#54af92ef .cell execution_count=94}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex29_good_kid(d):
    d = list(d)
    d[d.index(min(d))] += 1
    return math.prod(d)

assert [ex29_good_kid(t) for t in ([2, 2, 1, 2], [0, 1, 2], [4, 3, 2, 3, 4], [9] * 9)] == [16, 2, 432, 430467210]
for _ in range(500):
    e29 = [random.randint(0, 9) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 9))]
    assert ex29_good_kid(e29) == max(math.prod(e29[:i] + [e29[i] + 1] + e29[i + 1:]) for i in range(len(e29)))
```
:::


