**32. [M] Given a permutation `p` of $0..n-1$, build its **inverse** `inv` with `inv[p[i]] = i`. Then explain why `p[inv[v]] == v` for every $v$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
One pass writes, for each position $i$, the position $i$ into the cell named by the value $p[i]$: `inv[p[i]] = i`. Indexing by *value* is the frequency-array mindset (Pattern 5) applied to a permutation. Since $p$ is a bijection, every cell of `inv` is written exactly once; and `inv[v]` is the position where $v$ sits, so $p[\text{inv}[v]] = v$.

::: {#a0cb08c8 .cell execution_count=97}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex32_inverse(p):
    inv = [0] * len(p)
    for i, x in enumerate(p):
        inv[x] = i
    return inv

for _ in range(300):
    e32 = random.sample(range(9), 9)
    e32_inv = ex32_inverse(e32)
    assert all(e32_inv[e32[i]] == i and e32[e32_inv[i]] == i for i in range(9))
```
