**36. [M] [LC 274 · H-Index](https://leetcode.com/problems/h-index/){difficulty="Medium"}. The h-index is the largest $h$ such that at least $h$ papers have at least $h$ citations each. Compute it in $O(n)$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$h$ can never exceed $n$, so a paper with more than $n$ citations counts the same as one with exactly $n$: put it in bucket $n$ (**capped buckets**). Then scan $h$ from $n$ down with a running count of papers having at least $h$ citations; the first $h$ where that count reaches $h$ is the answer.

::: {#dcab39d5 .cell execution_count=101}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex36_h_index(c):
    n = len(c)
    cnt = [0] * (n + 1)
    for x in c:
        cnt[min(x, n)] += 1                       # cap: values above n collapse into bucket n
    at_least = 0
    for h in range(n, -1, -1):
        at_least += cnt[h]
        if at_least >= h:
            return h

assert ex36_h_index([3, 0, 6, 1, 5]) == 3 and ex36_h_index([1, 3, 1]) == 1
for _ in range(500):
    e36 = [random.randint(0, 12) for _ in range(random.randint(1, 9))]
    assert ex36_h_index(e36) == max(h for h in range(len(e36) + 1) if sum(x >= h for x in e36) >= h)
```
:::


