**38. [H] Given $n + 1$ integers, each in $[1, n]$, find a repeated value using the array itself as the marker, in $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ extra space, then restore the array.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
All values are positive, so the **sign** of a cell is a free one-bit flag (@sec-arr-inplace). For each element read $v = |a_i|$; if $a_v$ is already negative, $v$ was seen before and is a repeat; otherwise negate $a_v$ to mark "value $v$ seen". Indices $1..n$ exist because the array has $n + 1$ cells. Read every value through `abs`, because its cell may already be marked. Restore by taking absolute values. (LeetCode 287 forbids modifying the array; there the intended solution is cycle detection, a later topic.)

::: {#9d809a4d .cell execution_count=103}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex38_find_repeat(a):
    a = list(a)
    rep = None
    for i in range(len(a)):
        v = abs(a[i])                             # the cell may already carry a mark
        if a[v] < 0:
            rep = v if rep is None else rep
        else:
            a[v] = -a[v]
    restored = [abs(x) for x in a]
    return rep, restored

for _ in range(1000):
    e38_n = random.randint(1, 8)
    e38 = [random.randint(1, e38_n) for _ in range(e38_n + 1)]
    rep, back = ex38_find_repeat(e38)
    assert e38.count(rep) >= 2 and back == e38
```
:::


