**40. [H] Values lie in $[0, n)$. Return the value whose *second* occurrence comes first (or $-1$), using $O(1)$ extra space: mark by adding $n$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Walk the array left to right; the original value at $i$ is `a[i] % n` (the cell may already carry marks). If `a[v] >= n`, the value $v$ has been seen and this is its second occurrence, the earliest one, since we scan left to right; otherwise add $n$ to `a[v]`. Adding $n$ keeps the original value readable as the remainder: the encoding of @eq-arr-encode with a one-bit "new value". The magnitudes stay below $2n$.

::: {#b6d04529 .cell execution_count=105}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def ex40_first_repeat(a):
    a, n = list(a), len(a)
    for i in range(n):
        v = a[i] % n                              # the original value of a[i]
        if a[v] >= n:
            return v
        a[v] += n
    return -1

def ex40_brute(a):
    seen = set()
    for x in a:
        if x in seen:
            return x
        seen.add(x)
    return -1

for _ in range(1000):
    e40_n = random.randint(1, 8)
    e40 = [random.randint(0, e40_n - 1) for _ in range(e40_n)]
    assert ex40_first_repeat(e40) == ex40_brute(e40)
```
:::


