## Further reading

- [Dijkstra, EWD 831 — "Why numbering should start at zero"](https://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/EWD/transcriptions/EWD08xx/EWD831.html) — the one-page argument for half-open ranges and 0-based indexing behind @sec-arr-ranges.
- [Morin, *Open Data Structures* — Ch. 2: Array-Based Lists](https://opendatastructures.org/ods-python/2_Array_Based_Lists.html) — a free, rigorous treatment of growable arrays and the amortised analysis of resizing (@sec-arr-vec).
- [Laaksonen, *Competitive Programmer's Handbook*](https://cses.fi/book/book.pdf) — chapter 2 on time complexity and the operation budget, chapter 4 on dynamic arrays and other basic structures in C++.
- [Drepper, *What Every Programmer Should Know About Memory*](https://www.akkadia.org/drepper/cpumemory.pdf) — the long, authoritative explanation of caches, cache lines and prefetching behind @sec-arr-cache.
- [Wikipedia — Dynamic array](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_array) — growth factors used by real libraries and the geometric-series argument for amortised $O(1)$ appends.
- [cppreference — `std::vector`](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/vector) — complexity guarantees, `reserve` versus `resize`, and the iterator-invalidation rules.
- [Python wiki — Time Complexity](https://wiki.python.org/moin/TimeComplexity) — the cost of every `list`, `dict` and `set` operation in CPython, useful when reasoning about $O(1)$ versus $O(n)$ in Python.
